    "result_text": "All tests green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI agreed with both findings and resolved them by collapsing the soft-delete filter into a single definition, matching this module's single-definition idiom (`Household`, `PossibleDuplicate`).\n\n**New `ActiveOwners` helper** (`ActiveOwners.java`) \u2014 defines the \"ignore soft-deleted owners\" rule once: `ActiveOwners.list(repo)` returns the persisted owners not flagged deleted, in repository iteration order. Its javadoc names the three scans that share it.\n\n**Finding 1 (duplicated inline predicate)** \u2014 removed the copy-pasted `if (existing.isDeleted()) continue;` from both `Household.hasMember` and `PossibleDuplicate.matchOf`; both now iterate `ActiveOwners.list(...)`, so the exclusion is decided in one place.\n\n**Finding 2 (inconsistent treatment in `Household`)** \u2014 routed `Household.size` through the same filter so it no longer counts soft-deleted owners, making it consistent with its sibling `hasMember`. This is the coherent reading (a soft-deleted owner is gone from business rules, so it shouldn't inflate another owner's household size/membership points); it's unasserted by the acceptance tests, which stay green.\n\n**Scope I deliberately left alone** (noted so it's a conscious choice, not an oversight): the quota scans (`FlagBulkSignup`, `ValidateDailyOwnerLimit`, `ValidateCityCapacity`), `BuildOwner`'s derived counts (`countNamesakes`, customer-code dedup), and `ListOwners` still scan `findAll()`. Those are separate concerns the spec doesn't address, and the findings were scoped to the duplicate/identity and household-membership scans \u2014 so the `ActiveOwners` contract is documented to that scope rather than overclaiming a global \"active owners everywhere\" rule.\n\nBoth acceptance tests (`coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains`, `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`) pass and the build is green.",
