    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe per-file changes (Owner field + getter/setter, DeleteOwner soft-delete, schema across all four dialects, OpenAPI) are each in the right unit and read naturally. The problem is how the new \"ignore soft-deleted owners\" rule is placed.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 the soft-delete filter is a cross-cutting rule duplicated inline rather than defined once.**\nThis codebase is deliberately disciplined about single-definition rules: `Household` exists so the resolver, duplicate gate and builder \"all agree\" on the id, and `PossibleDuplicate` is the \"canonical soft-match rule \u2026 defined once so the create and fetch pipelines agree.\" Against that grain, the new rule is copy-pasted as an inline `if (existing.isDeleted()) continue;` into two separate `ownerRepository.findAll()` loops (`PossibleDuplicate.matchOf` at line 46 and `Household.hasMember` at line 79). \"Ignore soft-deleted owners when scanning owners\" is exactly the kind of cross-pipeline predicate this codebase centralizes. It belongs in one place \u2014 e.g. a shared helper filtering the `findAll()` stream, or a repository `findAllActive()` \u2014 so every scan honors it consistently rather than each loop re-deciding.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 the filter is applied inconsistently within `Household`, which is a coherence smell.**\n`Household.hasMember` (line 79) now skips deleted owners, but `Household.size` (lines 101\u2013107), which loops the very same `findAll()` by `householdId`, does not. Whether `size` *should* count deleted owners is a spec/correctness question I'm not judging \u2014 but having two sibling methods in one class scan the same collection with one honoring the deleted flag and the other silently not is precisely the inconsistency a scattered inline predicate produces, and precisely what a single shared filter (Finding 1) would prevent. A maintainer reading `Household` would not find this split natural. At minimum make the treatment uniform/explicit; better, route both through the one shared filter.\n\nCollapsing these into a single definition would resolve both findings and match the established idiom of the module.",
